<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrzyce</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pyritz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyrzyce, German Pyritz, is a town in the West Pomeranian Voivodeship of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>. In 1124 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg">Otto of Bamberg</a> baptised several thousand Pomeranians there, beginning the region's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pyritz">Treaty of Pyritz</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was signed there in 1493, and the town became part of Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrzyce.jpg" alt="Pyrzyce" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Redaktor01 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrzyce_-_Baszta_Sowia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
